24151:
Highest frequency component decides K_{\max} by Einstein relation K_{\max} = E_{\max} - \phi with E = h \nu = \hbar \omega
Here \omega_{\max} = 12 \times 10^{15} \text{ rad/s}, so E_{\max} = h \omega_{\max} / (2 \pi) = 6.6 \times 10^{-34} \times 12 \times 10^{15} / (2 \pi) \text{ J}
With 1 \text{ eV} = 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J} this is E_{\max} \approx 7.88 \text{ eV}, giving K_{\max} = 7.88 - 2.8 \approx 5.08 \text{ eV} \approx 5.1 \text{ eV}
This value matches option C → (C)

24152:
Mass flow of water is \dot{m} = 5.0 \text{ L/min} = 5.0 / 60 \text{ kg/s} = 1 / 12 \text{ kg/s} with \Delta T = 87 - 27 = 60^{\circ}\text{C}
Heat rate needed is P = \dot{m} s \Delta T = (1 / 12) \times 4200 \times 60 = 21000 \text{ J/s}
With heat of combustion q = 5.0 \times 10^4 \text{ J/g}, fuel rate is P / q = 21000 / (5.0 \times 10^4) = 0.42 \text{ g/s}
This rate matches option D → (D)

24153:
Parallel springs add as k_{\text{eq}} = k_1 + k_2 = 10 + 20 = 30 \text{ N/m}
Absolute errors add in a sum, so \Delta k_{\text{eq}} = \Delta k_1 + \Delta k_2 = 0.2 + 0.3 = 0.5 \text{ N/m}
Percentage error is 100 \times \Delta k_{\text{eq}} / k_{\text{eq}} = 100 \times 0.5 / 30 \approx 1.67\%
This percentage matches option C → (C)

24154:
Both input NOR gates have joined inputs, so they work as inverters giving X = \bar{A} and Y = \bar{B}
The middle NOR gate combines X and Y and the last tied NOR gate inverts again, so the net output is Output = \overline{A \cdot B}
That is the truth table of a NAND gate
This matches option B → (B)

24155:
Wave speed on a stretched string is v = \sqrt{T / \mu}, so transit time is t = L / v = L \sqrt{\mu / T}
Hence t_1 = L_A \sqrt{\mu_A / T} and t_2 = L_B \sqrt{\mu_B / T}, giving t_1 / t_2 = (L_A / L_B) \times \sqrt{\mu_A / \mu_B}
With L_A = 2.5 \text{ m}, L_B = 1.5 \text{ m}, \mu_A = 2 \times 10^{-4} \text{ kg/m} and \mu_B = 4 \times 10^{-4} \text{ kg/m}, the ratio is (2.5 / 1.5) \times (1 / \sqrt{2}) = 5 / (3 \sqrt{2}) \approx 1.18
This ratio matches option A → (A)

24156:
Continuity gives A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2, so with A_1 = 6 \text{ cm}^2 and A_2 = 3 \text{ cm}^2 one gets v_2 = 2 v_1
Bernoulli between A and B gives P_1 - P_2 = (1 / 2) \rho (v_2^2 - v_1^2) = \rho g h with h = 5 \text{ cm}
Thus (1 / 2) \times 3 v_1^2 = g h, so v_1 = \sqrt{2 g h / 3} = \sqrt{2 \times 1000 \times 5 / 3} = 100 / \sqrt{3} \text{ cm/s} and discharge Q = A_1 v_1 = 6 \times 100 / \sqrt{3} = 200 \sqrt{3} \text{ cm}^3/\text{s}
This discharge matches option A → (A)

24157:
Distances from the origin are r_A = \sqrt{4^2 + 4^2 + 2^2} = 6 \text{ m} and r_B = \sqrt{2^2 + 2^2 + 1^2} = 3 \text{ m}
Potential of Q = 10^{-8} \text{ C} is V = k Q / r with k = 9 \times 10^9 \text{ N m}^2/\text{C}^2
External work moving q = 2 \times 10^{-6} \text{ C} inward is W = q (V_B - V_A) = k Q q \times (1 / 3 - 1 / 6) = 9 \times 10^9 \times 10^{-8} \times 2 \times 10^{-6} / 6 = 30 \times 10^{-6} \text{ J}
This work matches option B → (B)

24161:
Equal lengths give L_{Al} = L_{steel} = 60 \text{ cm} at 30^{\circ}\text{C} with rise \Delta T = 100 - 30 = 70^{\circ}\text{C}
Linear expansion gives \Delta L = L_0 \alpha \Delta T for each rod, so total \Delta L = 60 \times 70 \times (24 \times 10^{-6} + 12 \times 10^{-6}) \text{ cm}
That is \Delta L = 4200 \times 36 \times 10^{-6} = 0.1512 \text{ cm} \approx 0.15 \text{ cm}, so final length is 120 + 0.15 = 120.15 \text{ cm}
This length matches option C → (C)

24163:
Force gives acceleration \vec{a} = \vec{F} / m = t^3 \hat{i} - 0.75 t \hat{j} = t^3 \hat{i} - (3 / 4) t \hat{j} in SI units
Starting from rest at origin, \vec{v} = \int_0^t \vec{a} dt = (t^4 / 4) \hat{i} - (3 t^2 / 8) \hat{j}, so at t = 2 \text{ s} one gets \vec{v} = 4 \hat{i} - (3 / 2) \hat{j} \text{ m/s}
Further \vec{r} = \int_0^t \vec{v} dt = (t^5 / 20) \hat{i} - (t^3 / 8) \hat{j}, so at t = 2 \text{ s} one gets \vec{r} = (8 / 5) \hat{i} - \hat{j} \text{ m}
This position matches option A → (A)

24164:
Head-on kinetic energy converts fully to electrostatic energy, so K = (1 / (4 \pi \varepsilon_0)) \times (2 e)(Z e) / r_0
Hence r_0 = (1 / (4 \pi \varepsilon_0)) \times 2 Z e^2 / K with Z = 79 and K = 7.7 \text{ MeV} = 7.7 \times 10^6 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19} \text{ J}
Putting numbers gives r_0 = 9 \times 10^9 \times 2 \times 79 \times (1.6 \times 10^{-19})^2 / (7.7 \times 10^6 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-19}) \approx 2.95 \times 10^{-14} \text{ m}
This distance matches option A → (A)

24165:
Dark fringes satisfy d \sin\theta = (n - 1 / 2) \lambda, so on a screen at distance D the position is y_n = (n - 1 / 2) \lambda D / d
For the third dark fringe n = 3 gives y_3 = 2.5 \lambda D / d with \lambda = 500 \times 10^{-9} \text{ m}, D = 1 \text{ m} and d = 0.1 \text{ cm} = 10^{-3} \text{ m}
This is y_3 = 2.5 \times 500 \times 10^{-9} \times 1 / 10^{-3} = 1.25 \times 10^{-3} \text{ m} = 1.25 \times 10^{-1} \text{ cm}
This position matches option D → (D)

